WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.984 --> 00:00:06.448
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.890
DR. BEVIL CONWAYIf I ask you to name
some colors, or the color of a banana,

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.790
you would tell me a
banana is yellow, right?

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.634
Well this is, like, weird
really, when you think about it.

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:17.550
I don't know about your
house, but in my house

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.920
the bananas are green when we buy them.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.940
And then they spend, you
know, a day being yellow.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.340
And then they're, like, brown
spotted and, eventually, black things

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.680
before we throw them out.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.330
So in their whole life span they've
spent very little time of their lives

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:32.230
being yellow.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.830
But this is the color we encode.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:35.100
It's the color we think of.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.060
And in fact, it's so
strong that it's the color

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.490
we project onto the world around us.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:46.460
So color, we know, is not simply
the light hitting our eyes.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.430
Because there's all of these, what we
can think of as cognitive influences

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.870
that shape what you
experience out there.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:02:01.280
My current favorite example of the
interaction between art practice

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.740
and the neuroscience of color is
really a close reading of Matisse.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.720
In many of his finished
canvases he actually

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:14.820
leaves these little white bits of an
unpainted canvas between painted marks.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.190
And it's a discovery he made.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:18.890
Because by introducing
this little white buffer

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:22.420
you protect from color
contrast-- color induction--

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.300
you protect the colored
marks so that they

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.280
remain about the same
in the finished painting

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.290
as they were when he first made them.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:33.080
So as he's painting in the studio, and
he wants his apple to stay that color,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.770
he can paint the background
lots of other colors,

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:39.560
and have the apple still stay that
color by just not painting next to it.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.820
Just leaving a little
bit of white around it.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.200
So color contrast is
the simple phenomenon

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:52.320
that is when you when you paint a
mark, the color of the painted mark

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.040
changes as you paint the
background around the mark.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.730
And this is a problem that new artists
struggle with-- old artists struggle

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:01.380
with.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.530
And that's because the
color of the background,

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:08.400
unconsciously, is changing the way
in which the color of the foreground

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.340
appears.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.230
And our brains do this
effortlessly and unconsciously.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.300
So much so that you don't
even think about the impact

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:21.100
of the surround in shaping your
experience of the object color

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.630
that you're attending.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:27.720
And that discovery, as it
were, by Matisse I think

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.170
came about because of,
you know, his struggle

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.350
in the studio working with paint.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:35.630
Working with a visual system
that has particular constraints

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:40.330
on the way in which it computes
light information to generate color.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.900
And the consequence on art
history is that, you know,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.820
it changed forever how
we think about art.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.010
How we think about paint.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.090
How we think about what paintings are.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:53.780
We see that legacy in Rothko, where
now he's gotten rid of everything

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.630
except paint and canvas.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.810
Just large fields of color.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.180
And at the time that
Matisse was working that

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.240
would have been considered
completely crazy.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.830
Like, literally-- like, I
don't know what that is.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:12.710
So you see, you know, the impact
of these choices on art practice.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:17.250
The impact those choices make on
the trajectory of our tradition.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.899
Western art history tradition.

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:25.090
But they also, in this wonderful
way, produce this other discovery.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:29.561
Which is that color is
divorced from shape.

00:04:29.561 --> 00:04:30.555
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:30.555 --> 00:04:33.770
So Matisse shows us--
and then is extended

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:38.676
through Rothko-- shows us that you can
take color completely out of shape.

00:04:38.676 --> 00:04:41.826
The banana's color doesn't have
to be attached to the banana.

00:04:41.826 --> 00:04:45.840
You can have a field of yellow,
you can have a shape of a banana.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.070
The fact that we experience
those things together

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.708
happens because of, probably,
something statistical

00:04:52.708 --> 00:04:54.332
about their co-occurrence in the world.

00:04:54.332 --> 00:04:58.290
Not because of something fundamental
about their yoking in our brains.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:01.332
Because if they were
fundamentally yoked in our brains,

00:05:01.332 --> 00:05:07.020
we would never be able to see the Rothko
as a field of red divorced from shape.

